+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed board changes
+
+## Context
+
+This decision builds on **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event** (HLD Key Design Decisions): every committed edit receives one authoritative event revision, and only the route by which that revision reaches browsers is at stake here.
+
+MarketBoard boards need committed changes visible to connected browsers within 15 seconds of commit, under 800 simultaneous browsers and bursts of 20 edits per second. Three delivery routes all meet that target, so freshness alone does not separate them. What separates them is who runs the connection path and what the board owes its vendor for it. Choosing wrongly binds the board's core update path — the thing every browser uses continuously during market hours — to a cost structure or an operational burden it cannot later shed cheaply.
+
+The delivery route also sizes three sibling nodes: the change store must know what its revisions must support, delivery must know what connection machinery it owns, and browser sync must know what reconnect behavior to build.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Freshness against the 15-second goal** *(minor)* — all three routes meet it, so this is context, not a differentiator; it matters only as a floor.
+- **Recurring operating cost** *(major)* — whether the board pays a per-connection or per-message fee for its primary update path, scaling with the 800-browser load.
+- **Provider coupling of the core update path** *(major)* — whether board behavior during market hours depends on a third party's protocol, SDK, availability, and pricing changes. Permits-self-hosting cuts both ways: neither is forbidden, so this weighs the exposure.
+- **Connection and reconnect operations burden** *(moderate)* — whether the board owns session capacity, reconnect handling, and missed-message recovery, as opposed to delegating that machinery.
+- **Operational simplicity of the stateless path** *(moderate)* — how much machinery the simplest operation carries: repeated reads, lag, cursor queries, or publication reconciliation.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers run a request/response loop: every few seconds each browser asks the server for changes since its last-seen revision cursor. The change store backs cursor queries ("revisions after X"); delivery stays plain HTTPS; no long-lived connection machinery exists server-side. The board's entire update path is stateless request handling it already knows how to run and scale horizontally.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Freshness (minor): **strong** — changes land within a few seconds of the poll interval.
+- Recurring cost (major): **strong** — no per-connection vendor fees; costs are ordinary server capacity.
+- Provider coupling (major): **strong** — the update path is the board's own HTTPS endpoint.
+- Connection/reconnect ops (moderate): **strong** — nothing to reconnect; a fresh poll is the recovery.
+- Stateless simplicity (moderate): **partial** — the operation is the simplest, but every browser repeats reads even on an unchanged board, and updates lag a few seconds behind commits.
+
+**Summary.** Lowest complexity and zero coupling, paid for in lag and continuous repeated reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The board runs its own WebSocket server. Delivery maintains socket sessions and publishes each committed revision to connected browsers as it commits. The change store retains a replayable revision history so any browser can catch up. Browser sync subscribes on connect, and after a disconnect reconnects and fetches the revisions it missed from the store. No vendor sits on the path.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Freshness (minor): **strong** — pushes land at commit time.
+- Recurring cost (major): **strong** — ordinary server capacity; no per-connection fees.
+- Provider coupling (major): **strong** — the update path is entirely the board's.
+- Connection/reconnect ops (moderate): **weak** — the board owns connection capacity, session liveness, and reconnect/catch-up correctness.
+- Stateless simplicity (moderate): **weak** — long-lived connections, session state, and replay obligations replace the simple request loop.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, cheap, uncoupled updates, paid for in connection and reconnect operations the board must own.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party managed realtime provider (pub/sub with client SDKs) sits between the change store and browsers. Delivery wraps the provider's publication API; the store maintains reconciliation so the provider's state never diverges from authoritative revisions; browsers subscribe through the provider's SDK and rely on its reconnection behavior. The board sheds socket operations in exchange for a subscription.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Freshness (minor): **strong** — the provider's core product is low-latency push.
+- Recurring cost (major): **weak** — subscription and usage fees scale with the 800-browser load.
+- Provider coupling (major): **weak** — market-hours behavior and pricing depend on the provider.
+- Connection/reconnect ops (moderate): **strong** — delegated to the provider's client SDKs.
+- Stateless simplicity (moderate): **partial** — no self-run sockets, but publication reconciliation between store and provider replaces it with different machinery.
+
+**Summary.** Fastest route to low latency with the least connection work, paid for in recurring fees and coupling of the core update path to a vendor.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets. The three options separate on two axes: who owns connection operations (self-hosted owns it; polling avoids it; the provider delegates it) and who runs the update path (self-hosted and polling run it; the provider does). On the two major criteria — recurring cost and provider coupling — self-hosted and polling both fit strongly, and the provider fits weakly; the tie breaks on the freshness/simplicity trade. Polling's simplicity is real but its lag and continuous repeated reads are paid on every browser on every poll, while self-hosting's connection-ops burden is bounded machinery that reconnect-and-catch-up design addresses once. The freshness goal does not force the choice — polling meets it — so this decision turns on preferring no vendor on the core update path and no usage-priced fees over minimum operational machinery, which the infrastructure tenet explicitly permits: self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both allowed, so the lean is the board's to take. It also takes against simplicity, accepting the connection and reconnect operations burden deliberately.
+
+Committing forecloses the provider's delegated reconnection and caps-free operation at large scale, and obligates downstream: the change store must keep replayable revisions, delivery must manage sessions and publish reliably, browser sync must reconnect and catch up missed revisions from the store. If the connection-ops burden later proves heavier than the value of subsecond updates, five-second polling remains the fallback route — the one authoritative revision survives any of the three.
